# Brave's rpm-md repository, VENDORED (D-2/C-1, 2026-09-03).
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS COMMITTED. It used to be curled from
# https://brave-browser-rpm-release.s3.brave.com/brave-browser.repo at build time,
# seconds before `dnf install -y brave-browser` resolved a bare package name
# against it. Both the repo DEFINITION and the package VERSION were therefore
# whatever Brave was serving that minute: two ISOs built from identical source on
# different days contained different browsers, and there was no record anywhere in
# the tree of which Brave shipped in which image. That is the same reasoning that
# already vendors config/flatpak/flathub.flatpakrepo -- committed rather than
# curled so the trust root we ship is auditable in git history and the build does
# not depend on a third party's uptime.
#
# The GPG key is vendored alongside as brave-core.asc and installed into
# /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/, so `gpgkey=` is a file:// URL: nothing about the trust root is
# fetched over the network at build time.
#
# This file and the key are BOTH removed inside the same RUN that installs Brave.
# They must not ship (D-6): /etc is machine state on bootc and is three-way merged
# on every upgrade, so a live third-party rpm-md repo left in /etc/yum.repos.d is
# permanently sticky on installed machines and is a standing invitation to
# `rpm-ostree install`, which is what makes a machine silently stop upgrading.
#
# TO BUMP BRAVE: change ARG BRAVE_VERSION / ARG BRAVE_RELEASE in
# images/kde/Containerfile. Only re-fetch this file or the key if Brave changes
# its repository layout or rotates its signing key.
[brave-browser]
name=Brave Browser
enabled=1
gpgcheck=1
gpgkey=file:///etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-brave-browser
baseurl=https://brave-browser-rpm-release.s3.brave.com/$basearch
